## Core Requirements
- Never bypass git hooks with `--no-verify` or `-n`
- Follow conventional commit format with corresponding emojis
- Use descriptive branch names
- Include issue references
- Run pre-commit checks
- Protect main branches from direct pushes
- Sign commits and tags when possible
- Use semantic versioning for releases

## Patterns and Examples
```bash
# ❌ Bad: Bypassing pre-commit hooks
git commit -m "fix bug" --no-verify
git commit -m "fix bug" -n

# ✅ Good: Allowing hooks to run
git commit -m "fix: 🐛 resolve issue with login form validation"
```

```bash
# ❌ Bad: Non-descriptive commit message
git commit -m "fixes"
git commit -m "update code"
git commit -m "WIP"

# ❌ Bad: Missing emoji
git commit -m "feat: add user authentication flow"

# ✅ Good: Conventional commit format with emojis
git commit -m "feat: ✨ add user authentication flow"
git commit -m "fix: 🐛 resolve race condition in data fetching"
git commit -m "docs: 📝 update API documentation"
git commit -m "style: 💄 improve button styling"
git commit -m "refactor: ♻️ simplify error handling logic"
git commit -m "perf: ⚡️ optimize database queries"
git commit -m "test: ✅ add unit tests for auth service"
git commit -m "build: 👷 update build configuration"
git commit -m "ci: 🔧 fix CI pipeline"
git commit -m "chore: 🔨 update dependencies"
git commit -m "revert: ⏪ revert last commit"
git commit -m "feat: 💥 ✨ add breaking API changes"
git commit -m "security: 🔒 fix XSS vulnerability"
git commit -m "deps: ⬆️ update dependencies"
git commit -m "a11y: ♿ improve screen reader support"
git commit -m "i18n: 🌐 add Spanish translations"
git commit -m "config: ⚙️ update webpack configuration"
git commit -m "init: 🎉 initial commit"
```

```bash
# ✅ Good: Using scopes for additional context
git commit -m "feat(auth): ✨ add user authentication flow"
git commit -m "fix(api): 🐛 resolve race condition in data fetching"
git commit -m "style(button): 💄 improve hover state styling"
git commit -m "perf(db): ⚡️ optimize query performance"
```

```bash
# ❌ Bad: Branch naming
git checkout -b fix
git checkout -b update-stuff

# ✅ Good: Descriptive branch names
git checkout -b feature/123-user-authentication
git checkout -b fix/456-login-validation
git checkout -b docs/update-api-docs
```

```bash
# ✅ Good: Tagging with semantic versioning
git tag -a v1.0.0 -m "Initial stable release"
git tag -a v1.1.0 -m "Added new features"
git tag -a v1.1.1 -m "Fixed bugs in v1.1.0"
git tag -a v2.0.0 -m "Breaking changes to API"
```

```bash
# ✅ Good: Rebase workflow to keep branches updated
git checkout feature/123-user-authentication
git fetch origin
git rebase origin/main
git push --force-with-lease
```

```bash
# ✅ Good: Managing large files with Git LFS
git lfs track "*.png"
git lfs track "*.zip"
git add .gitattributes
git commit -m "chore: 🔨 configure Git LFS for binary files"
```

## Commit Types
| Type | Emoji | Description |
|---|----|---|
| feat | ✨ | A new feature |
| fix | 🐛 | A bug fix |
| docs | 📝 | Documentation only changes |
| style | 💄 | Changes that do not affect the meaning of the code (white-space, formatting, etc) |
| refactor | ♻️ | A code change that neither fixes a bug nor adds a feature |
| perf | ⚡️ | A code change that improves performance |
| test | ✅ | Adding missing tests or correcting existing tests |
| build | 👷 | Changes that affect the build system or external dependencies |
| ci | 🔧 | Changes to our CI configuration files and scripts |
| chore | 🔨 | Other changes that don't modify src or test files |
| revert | ⏪ | Reverts a previous commit |
| breaking | 💥 | Indicates a breaking change (used with other types) |
| security | 🔒 | Security-related changes or vulnerability fixes |
| deps | ⬆️ | Dependency updates or downgrades |
| a11y | ♿ | Accessibility improvements |
| i18n | 🌐 | Internationalization and localization changes |
| config | ⚙️ | Configuration changes |
| init | 🎉 | Initial commit or project bootstrap |

## Commit Scopes

Scopes can be added in parentheses after the commit type to provide additional context about which part of the codebase is being affected:

```
feat(auth): ✨ add user authentication flow
fix(api): 🐛 resolve race condition in data fetching
```

Common scopes include:
- Module or feature names (auth, api, dashboard)
- Component names (button, modal, form)
- Areas of concern (db, cache, security)
- Platforms or environments (ios, android, web)

## Branch Protection Rules

Protected branches (main, master, develop) must follow these rules:

- Direct pushes are not allowed
- Changes must be introduced via pull requests
- Pull requests require at least one approved review
- All required CI checks must pass
- Branch must be up-to-date with base branch
- Force pushes are prohibited (except for repository administrators in emergency situations)
- Branch deletion is prohibited

Example GitHub branch protection configuration:
```yaml
branches:
  - name: main
    protection:
      required_pull_request_reviews:
        required_approving_review_count: 1
        dismiss_stale_reviews: true
      required_status_checks:
        strict: true
        contexts: ["build", "test", "lint"]
      enforce_admins: true
      restrictions: null
```

## Pull/Merge Request Guidelines

All PRs/MRs must follow these guidelines:

- Use a descriptive title that summarizes the change
- Use the provided PR template
- Reference related issues with "Fixes #123" or "Relates to #123"
- Keep changes focused and limited in scope (max 500 lines changed when possible)
- Include tests for new functionality
- Update documentation as necessary
- Assign appropriate reviewers
- Add relevant labels

PR titles should follow conventional commit format:
```
feat: add user authentication flow
fix: resolve race condition in data fetching
```

## Tagging & Release Management

Tags and releases must follow semantic versioning (MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH):

- MAJOR version: incompatible API changes
- MINOR version: backwards-compatible functionality
- PATCH version: backwards-compatible bug fixes

All tags should be:
- Annotated (`git tag -a`)
- Signed when possible (`git tag -s`)
- Include a clear message describing the release
- Follow the format `vX.Y.Z` (e.g., `v1.2.3`)

Release process:
1. Update version in appropriate files (package.json, etc.)
2. Update CHANGELOG.md
3. Commit changes with `chore(release): 🔨 bump version to X.Y.Z`
4. Create an annotated tag
5. Push commits and tags
6. Create a GitHub/GitLab release with release notes

## Rebase vs. Merge Strategy

The preferred strategy for incorporating changes is rebasing:

- Feature branches should regularly rebase on the latest main branch
- Use `git rebase` instead of `git merge` to maintain a linear history
- When pushing after a rebase, use `git push --force-with-lease` to avoid overwriting others' work
- Squash commits before merging to main when appropriate

When to rebase:
- Updating feature branches with latest main changes
- Cleaning up commit history before a PR
- Resolving conflicts with main branch

When to merge:
- Merging a completed feature into main (via PR)
- Merging between long-lived branches

## GitLab/GitHub Specific Workflows

### GitHub Workflow:
- Use GitHub Flow (feature branches → PRs → main)
- Configure GitHub Actions for CI/CD
- Use GitHub Issues for task tracking
- Enable Dependabot for dependency updates
- Use GitHub Projects for project management

### GitLab Workflow:
- Use GitLab Flow (feature branches → MRs → main → production)
- Configure GitLab CI/CD pipelines
- Use GitLab Issues for task tracking
- Enable GitLab Dependency Scanning
- Use GitLab Boards for project management

## Large File Handling

For binary or large files:

- Use Git LFS for files over 5MB
- Track binary files patterns in .gitattributes:
  ```
  *.png filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
  *.jpg filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
  *.pdf filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
  *.zip filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
  ```
- Initialize Git LFS with `git lfs install`
- Verify with `git lfs status` and `git lfs ls-files`
- Consider excluding large generated files via .gitignore

## Squashing History

Squash commits in these situations:

- When merging feature branches with multiple work-in-progress commits
- When many small, related changes were made incrementally
- When the individual commits don't provide meaningful context

Don't squash:
- Commits that represent distinct, logical changes
- Commits from multiple contributors (preserves authorship)
- Commits on main or other shared branches

Example squash command:
```bash
# Squash the last 3 commits
git reset --soft HEAD~3
git commit -m "feat: ✨ add complete user authentication flow"
```

## Git Submodules & Subtrees

When working with external dependencies:

### Submodules:
- Add with `git submodule add <repository> <path>`
- Initialize with `git submodule init`
- Update with `git submodule update --recursive`
- Include submodules when cloning with `git clone --recursive`

### Subtrees:
- Add with `git subtree add --prefix=<path> <repository> <ref>`
- Update with `git subtree pull --prefix=<path> <repository> <ref>`
- Contribute changes with `git subtree push --prefix=<path> <repository> <branch>`

## Debugging Tools

Use these Git tools for debugging:

- `git bisect`: Binary search to find the commit that introduced a bug
  ```bash
  git bisect start
  git bisect bad # Current commit has the bug
  git bisect good v1.0.0 # This version didn't have the bug
  # Git will checkout commits for you to test
  git bisect good # or git bisect bad after testing
  git bisect reset # Return to original state
  ```

- `git blame`: Find who modified a line and in which commit
  ```bash
  git blame -L 10,20 file.js # Show who changed lines 10-20
  ```

- `git reflog`: Recover lost commits or branches
  ```bash
  git reflog # See all recent ref updates
  git checkout HEAD@{2} # Go to specific reflog entry
  ```

## Performance Considerations

For large repositories:

- Use shallow clones when full history isn't needed:
  ```bash
  git clone --depth=1 <repository>
  ```

- Use partial clones to exclude large objects:
  ```bash
  git clone --filter=blob:none <repository>
  ```

- Use sparse checkout for specific directories:
  ```bash
  git clone --sparse <repository>
  git sparse-checkout set <directory>
  ```

- Optimize local repository:
  ```bash
  git gc --aggressive
  git prune
  ```

- Use `--no-merged` to find stale branches:
  ```bash
  git branch --no-merged main
  ```

## Hook Enforcement
- Pre-commit hooks must always run
- Pre-push hooks must always run
- Git hooks should never be bypassed
- Fix issues instead of bypassing validation

